{"kind":"propose","text":"DECISION PROPOSAL — MEASURE MAX-FAN-OUT AS INCREMENTAL COST.\n\nDecision. The max-fan-out target measures the additional append latency caused by adding causal bases, not the total append latency. Total append latency includes signing, Git publication, verification and folding that a one-base append already pays. Calling that fixed cost a fan-out miss hides which dimension needs repair and makes a fan-out optimization unable to satisfy its own target.\n\nBound. On the contract linear fixture at depth 50,000, one actor, the same exact head and a quiet measurement window, run at least seven alternating rounds at fan-outs 1, 8, 16, 64 and 256. Pair each maximum-width sample with its round one-base sample. PREVIEW passes when the median added latency at 64 bases is no more than 15.75 ms. FIRST-PRODUCTION passes when the median added latency at 256 bases is no more than 63.75 ms. These are the same ceiling of 0.25 ms per additional causal base. Retain and report every width, the ranges, fixture digest, head, environment and correctness digests so a nonlinear jump or invalid result cannot disappear into the endpoint. No negative adjustment is credited beyond zero cost.\n\nWhy this number. The accepted depth-50,000 evidence reports 0.22 ms per added base across 1, 16, 64 and 256, which is about 13.9 ms at 64 and 56.1 ms at 256 and therefore passes with small visible headroom. The ceiling is narrow enough to catch a roughly 14 percent regression at the observed slope and caps the whole FIRST-PRODUCTION fan-out increment at about 64 ms instead of permitting 50 ms for every base.\n\nIndependent reproduction on current main adda28c8 used the committed gitseq.performance/v1 generator, seed 632, linear one-actor fixture and exact fixture digest cfad716534d692f831f0091383953a7800eae320978ed0babe39ef946004f5e4. Seven alternating rounds at depth 1,000 produced medians of 527.782, 514.068, 508.786, 507.960 and 519.902 ms at fan-outs 1, 8, 16, 64 and 256. The paired median 256-minus-1 delta was 3.291 ms, or 0.0129 ms per added base. Median allocation rose from 84.428 MiB to 85.899 MiB. Every sample had equal trusted and projected correctness digests. The attached JSONL is the raw 35-sample record.\n\nThe depth-50,000 exploratory rerun also showed why absolute latency must remain separate: single one-base and 256-base samples took 12.330 s and 15.684 s while load averages were 6 to 9. Those unpaired, noisy points are not used to claim an incremental rate. The previously accepted quiet-window evidence, including 764.0 versus 773.7 ms medians on the 311-record workroom and the depth-50,000 slope, is the governing distribution until a retained paired rerun replaces it.\n\nSeparate product goal. The absolute 50 ms append budget remains useful: a person should not wait on the fixed append pipeline merely because fan-out is cheap. It becomes a fixed one-base submit-ack target under a separate bounded request. Current evidence misses it: 764.0 ms at one base in the workroom evidence and 527.8 ms at depth 1,000 in this reproduction.\n\nContracts. This decision changes measurement attribution only. It does not defer or remove actor signing, sequencer admission, verified-ref compare-and-swap, signature or bound verification, complete fold semantics, trusted-versus-projected digest equality, or atomic publication. A faster path that weakens any of them does not meet either target. This proposal governs only after an authorized ratifier adopts it."}